# Africa batch (1905-2023): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"af-nandi": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted the Uganda Railway and its telegraph secure across Nandi land after a decade of raids; the Nandi wanted the line and the soldiers gone. British officer Richard Meinertzhagen shot the Nandi orkoiyot Koitalel Arap Samoei at a meeting arranged as a truce in October 1905, and Nandi resistance collapsed within weeks. The Nandi were confined to a reserve and the Uasin Gishu plateau went to white settlers, the pattern for the Kenya highlands.",
 now="The killing cleared the Uasin Gishu plateau for white settlement between 1906 and 1920 and fixed the reserve system that separated Kenya's land into African and settler zones until independence in 1963.",
 story="The Uganda Railway and its telegraph crossed Nandi land in western Kenya, and Nandi fighters raided both for about ten years. Britain mounted five expeditions against them. On 19 October 1905 British officer Richard Meinertzhagen met the Nandi orkoiyot, or spiritual leader, Koitalel Arap Samoei under a flag of truce and shot him dead with his escort.",
 why="Britain wanted the railway, the telegraph and the highlands secure for settlement. The Nandi had held out longer than any other people in Kenya.",
 trig="The truce meeting at Ket-ny-barak on 19 October 1905.",
 then="Nandi resistance ended within weeks. The Nandi were moved to a reserve in 1906, and the Uasin Gishu plateau was opened to white settlers.",
 fact="Meinertzhagen was sent home over the killing after an inquiry, and later became a noted ornithologist whose bird specimens were found to include stolen museum skins."),
"af190531": dict(
 bluf="Peoples of southern Tanganyika rose to end German forced cotton growing, taxes and corporal punishment, trusting a prophet's water to turn bullets to water. Germany crushed the rising within two years with a scorched-earth campaign, and famine killed somewhere between 75,000 and 300,000 people. The disaster drove Germany to reform its colonial administration, and the rising later became the founding story of Tanzanian unity across ethnic lines.",
 now="The rising forced the German reforms of 1907 and gave Tanzania a shared anti-colonial memory that cut across its 120 ethnic groups, which Tanzanian President Julius Nyerere used to build a single national identity after 1961.",
 story="German East Africa forced villagers in the south to grow cotton for export on communal plots under corporal punishment. In July 1905 Matumbi villagers uprooted cotton, and the rising spread to the Ngoni and some twenty other peoples behind the prophet Kinjikitile Ngwale, whose 'maji', or water, was said to turn German bullets to water. German forces hanged Kinjikitile in August 1905 and burned crops and villages across the south until 1907.",
 why="Forced cotton labour, a hut tax and the whip came first. The maji cult gave scattered peoples a single cause and a promise of protection.",
 trig="Matumbi villagers uprooted the cotton at Nandete in July 1905.",
 then="Famine followed the burning; estimates of the dead run from 75,000 to 300,000, most from hunger. Berlin created a Colonial Office in 1907 and shifted to African cash crops. In November 2023 German President Frank-Walter Steinmeier asked forgiveness in Tanzania for the German crimes of the war.",
 fact="'Maji' is Swahili for water. Fighters sprinkled it on their bodies before battle in the belief that German bullets would turn to water."),
"af-dernburg": dict(
 bluf="German Colonial Secretary Bernhard Dernburg wanted colonies that paid their way after the Maji Maji and Herero wars had cost Berlin money and scandal. He created a Colonial Office in 1907, built railways and promoted African cash crops in place of rule by the whip. German East Africa gained the Central Line to Lake Tanganyika and a cotton and coffee economy; one reading calls it reform, the other a more efficient extraction.",
 now="The railways and cash-crop economy built between 1907 and 1914 became the base of Tanganyika's economy under British rule and after independence, which is why Tanzania's export crops and transport lines still follow the German map.",
 story="The colonial wars in East and South-West Africa cost hundreds of millions of marks and became scandals in the Reichstag, and the January 1907 election was fought over colonial policy. Banker Bernhard Dernburg became the first German Colonial Secretary in May 1907 and argued that colonies should be run as businesses resting on African farmers. In Dar es Salaam, Governor Albrecht von Rechenberg favoured African peasant production over settler plantations.",
 why="The wars had made force expensive and unpopular at home. Dernburg and Rechenberg believed African farmers would produce more and cost less than settler estates.",
 then="The Central Line reached Lake Tanganyika at Kigoma in 1914. Cotton, coffee and sisal exports grew. One reading holds the programme was real reform, citing the end of forced cotton and the peasant policy; the other holds it was exploitation by other means, citing the hut tax that still drove Africans into wage labour.",
 fact="The 1907 Reichstag vote became known as the 'Hottentot election' after the Nama people Germany had just fought in South-West Africa."),
"af190832": dict(
 bluf="British consul Roger Casement and journalist E. D. Morel set out to end Belgian King Leopold II's personal rule over the Congo Free State by exposing its rubber regime of mutilation, hostage-taking and mass death. The Belgian parliament annexed the Congo as a state colony in November 1908. One of the first modern human-rights campaigns had removed a sovereign from his private empire; Belgian rule stayed extractive until 1960.",
 now="The campaign established the model of international pressure through documented atrocity. Belgian rule left Congo with fewer than twenty African university graduates at independence in 1960, which shaped the state's collapse that followed.",
 story="Belgian King Leopold II had ruled the Congo Free State as personal property since 1885 and ran it on forced rubber collection enforced by mutilation and hostage-taking. British consul Roger Casement's report of 1904 documented the system, and journalist E. D. Morel's Congo Reform Association campaigned in Britain and the United States. On 15 November 1908 the Belgian parliament annexed the territory as the Belgian Congo.",
 why="Casement's report gave the atrocities official standing, and Morel's campaign made Leopold's rule a diplomatic liability for Belgium.",
 then="Belgium ruled the Congo until 1960, with regulated labour and company concessions in place of Leopold's rubber terror. In June 2020 Belgian King Philippe expressed his 'deepest regrets' for the wounds of the colonial period.",
 fact="Casement, knighted for his Congo and Amazon reports, was hanged by Britain in 1916 for seeking German help for the Irish rising."),
"af191033": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted to reconcile British and Boer whites eight years after the Boer War and hand them a self-governing dominion. The Union of South Africa was created on 31 May 1910 on terms that reserved the vote to white men in three of its four provinces. White unity was bought by excluding Black Africans from power, and the 1913 Natives Land Act that followed fixed the racial land map that apartheid later completed.",
 now="The 1913 land law confined African ownership to about 7 per cent of the country, later 13 per cent, and the dispute over who owns South Africa's land runs through its politics into the 2020s.",
 story="Britain had defeated the Boer republics in 1902. In 1910 it merged the Cape, Natal, the Transvaal and the Orange Free State into the Union of South Africa, a self-governing dominion. Only white men could vote except in the Cape, which kept a qualified franchise. The Natives Land Act of 1913 restricted African land ownership to about 7 per cent of the country.",
 why="Britain wanted a loyal white dominion and the gold mines running. Boer leaders accepted union on condition that the vote stayed white outside the Cape.",
 then="Black leaders founded the South African Native National Congress in January 1912, renamed the African National Congress in 1923. The land act forced African tenant farmers off white-owned land into reserves and wage labour.",
 fact="The Union's two former Boer generals, Louis Botha and Jan Smuts, became its first prime ministers."),
"af-tanga": dict(
 bluf="Britain landed about 8,000 Indian Army troops at Tanga in November 1914 to take German East Africa's northern port in a single stroke. German commander Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck's force of about 1,000 counterattacked, bee swarms disturbed by gunfire broke up British units, and the British re-embarked after three days leaving their weapons on the beach. A four-year campaign followed in which a small mobile force tied down armies many times its size.",
 now="The captured rifles, machine guns and ammunition from Tanga supplied the German force for months and made possible the campaign that kept some 150,000 Allied troops in East Africa until November 1918.",
 story="Indian Expeditionary Force B, about 8,000 men, landed near Tanga on 2 November 1914 to seize German East Africa's northern railhead. German commander Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck brought about 1,000 German officers and African askari by rail and counterattacked on 4 November. Bees disturbed by gunfire stung both sides and broke up British units. The British re-embarked on 5 November and left their equipment behind.",
 why="Britain wanted the colony taken quickly before it could threaten Kenya. The landing force was untrained and its commander gave the Germans warning by delaying.",
 then="Lettow-Vorbeck's force took 16 machine guns, hundreds of rifles and 600,000 rounds. The East Africa campaign ran until two weeks after the armistice.",
 fact="British propaganda later claimed the Germans had used the bees on purpose; the swarms stung both sides and there is no evidence of a plan."),
"af191434": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted German East Africa taken and Germany wanted British troops tied down far from Europe. German commander Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck's force of German officers and African askari evaded far larger armies across Tanganyika, Kenya and Mozambique for four years and surrendered only after the armistice. The campaign held some 150,000 Allied troops, consumed more than a million African porters, of whom perhaps 100,000 died, and transferred Tanganyika from Germany to Britain.",
 now="The campaign tied down 150,000 Allied troops for four years, killed perhaps 100,000 African porters and transferred Tanganyika from Germany to Britain, which fixed the map of East Africa until independence.",
 story="The war reached East Africa in August 1914. German commander Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck led a force that peaked at about 3,000 Germans and 12,000 askari on a four-year chase through Tanganyika, Kenya and Mozambique against British, Indian, South African, Belgian and Portuguese forces. Both sides moved supplies on the backs of more than a million African porters. He surrendered on 25 November 1918 after learning of the armistice in Europe.",
 why="Germany wanted British forces held away from Europe, and Britain wanted the colony and its coast. Neither side could feed an army in the bush without conscripted carriers.",
 then="Hundreds of thousands of porters and civilians died of disease, hunger and exhaustion; the British Carrier Corps alone recorded over 40,000 deaths. Famine and influenza followed the armies across the region in 1918-19.",
 fact="The porters who died are the least recorded dead of the war; most have no name in any register."),
"af-surrender": dict(
 bluf="German commander Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck wanted to keep his force in the field and Allied troops occupied until Germany won in Europe. He learned of the armistice from a captured British dispatch and surrendered at Abercorn in Northern Rhodesia on 25 November 1918, the last German force of the war, unbeaten in battle. Germany made him a hero; the hundreds of thousands of Africans who died carrying his and his enemies' supplies went unrecorded.",
 now="The surrender ended German rule in Africa, and the Allies divided the colonies as League of Nations mandates in 1919-22, which drew the borders of today's Tanzania, Rwanda, Burundi, Namibia, Togo and Cameroon.",
 story="German commander Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck's force of German officers and African askari had evaded far larger British forces for four years, living off the land and moving through Mozambique into Northern Rhodesia. He learned of the armistice from a captured British dispatch and surrendered at Abercorn, now Mbala in Zambia, on 25 November 1918, two weeks after the war ended in Europe. His campaign cost hundreds of thousands of African lives, most of them porters and civilians, through disease, famine and forced labour.",
 why="The armistice of 11 November had ended the war, and Lettow-Vorbeck learned of it two weeks late from a captured message.",
 then="The Allies divided Germany's colonies among themselves as League of Nations mandates. Lettow-Vorbeck was paraded through Berlin in March 1919 as the one undefeated German general.",
 fact="His campaign is still taught in military colleges as a model of guerrilla war; the porters who carried it are absent from most of the lessons."),
"af-tanganyika": dict(
 bluf="The Allies wanted Germany's colonies and US President Woodrow Wilson wanted them held in trust rather than annexed. The Treaty of Versailles gave most of German East Africa to Britain as a League of Nations mandate, confirmed in 1922, and Ruanda-Urundi to Belgium. The mandate was colonial rule in practice, and its reporting duty to the League, then the UN, gave Tanganyikans an international forum they used to press for independence by 1961.",
 now="The mandate's borders are today's Tanzania, Rwanda and Burundi, and the idea of international trusteeship shaped the UN's later push for decolonisation.",
 story="The Treaty of Versailles of 1919 stripped Germany of its colonies. Britain received most of German East Africa as a League of Nations mandate, renamed Tanganyika, with the mandate formally conferred in 1922. Belgium received Ruanda-Urundi. Britain ruled through chiefs under 'indirect rule' with far less white settlement than in Kenya.",
 why="The Allies wanted the colonies, and US President Woodrow Wilson's principles obliged them to call the arrangement trusteeship under League oversight.",
 then="Tanganyika became a UN trust territory in 1946, which gave its nationalists a hearing in New York. Tanzanian President Julius Nyerere's party used that forum on the road to independence in 1961.",
 fact="Germany's East African colony had been larger than Germany itself."),
"af193536": dict(
 bluf="Italian dictator Benito Mussolini wanted a colonial empire, revenge for Italy's defeat at Adwa in 1896 and a display of fascist strength. His armies invaded Ethiopia in October 1935, used mustard gas on soldiers and civilians, and took Addis Ababa in May 1936 while the League of Nations imposed sanctions that excluded oil. One of Africa's two independent states fell, and the League's failure showed every aggressor that collective security had no force behind it.",
 now="The League's failure over Ethiopia showed German Chancellor Adolf Hitler that aggression would go unpunished, and it pushed Italy into alliance with Germany by 1936-37.",
 story="Italy invaded Ethiopia on 3 October 1935 from Eritrea and Somaliland with about 400,000 troops and used mustard gas on soldiers and civilians. Ethiopian Emperor Haile Selassie went into exile after Addis Ababa fell on 5 May 1936 and addressed the League of Nations in Geneva on 30 June 1936 with the warning 'It is us today. It will be you tomorrow.' The League's sanctions left oil and the Suez Canal untouched.",
 why="Mussolini wanted revenge for Adwa, an empire linking Eritrea and Somaliland and a war to display fascist power. Britain and France valued Italy against Germany above the League.",
 then="Italy left the League in December 1937 and drew closer to Germany. British and Ethiopian forces freed the country in 1941 and restored Haile Selassie.",
 fact="Ethiopia and Liberia were the only African states free of European rule in 1935."),
"af194837": dict(
 bluf="South African Prime Minister D. F. Malan's National Party set out to secure Afrikaner power and white supremacy by law after winning the whites-only election of May 1948. It built a system of racial classification, separate residence, pass controls and later ethnic 'homelands' that stripped Black South Africans of citizenship, and held it until 1994. South Africa became an international pariah, and the struggle against apartheid became a defining human-rights cause of the century.",
 now="Apartheid ended in 1994, and the land, wealth and skills distribution it built left South Africa among the most unequal countries on Earth into the 2020s.",
 story="South African Prime Minister D. F. Malan's National Party won the whites-only election of 26 May 1948 on a platform of apartheid, or separateness. Laws followed that classified every person by race, banned mixed marriages, assigned neighbourhoods by race and forced Black South Africans to carry passes. From 1970 the state assigned Black citizens to ethnic 'homelands' and removed their South African citizenship.",
 why="Afrikaner nationalism and fear of the Black majority came first. White workers wanted protection from competition, and mining and farming wanted cheap Black labour without political rights.",
 then="Resistance ran from the 1960 Sharpeville massacre to the 1976 Soweto uprising, and sanctions and sporting bans followed from the 1960s. Negotiations began in 1990 and the first democratic election came in 1994.",
 fact="The National Party won in 1948 with fewer votes than its opponents; rural constituencies with small electorates carried it."),
"af195238": dict(
 bluf="Kikuyu fighters of the Kenya Land and Freedom Army took up arms in 1952 to recover land taken by white settlers and to win political rights. Britain declared an emergency, detained about 150,000 people, used torture and hanged more than 1,000, and defeated the fighters by 1956. The cost convinced Britain it could hold Kenya only by force, and independence followed in December 1963.",
 now="The war showed Britain that settler rule in Kenya could be held only by force, which brought independence in 1963. In 2013 Britain paid compensation to more than 5,000 survivors of the camps.",
 story="White settlers held the best farmland in the Kenyan highlands, and Kikuyu squatters and workers were landless and shut out of politics. Fighters of the Kenya Land and Freedom Army attacked settlers and African loyalists. Britain declared an emergency on 20 October 1952, detained about 150,000 people in camps and villages and used torture widely. The fighters were defeated by 1956.",
 why="Land came first: settlers held the highlands and Kikuyu squatters were being evicted. Political exclusion gave the grievance no legal outlet.",
 trig="Britain declared the emergency on 20 October 1952 and arrested Kenyan nationalist Jomo Kenyatta and other leaders.",
 then="Britain hanged more than 1,000 fighters and held detainees without trial into 1959. Kenya became independent on 12 December 1963 under Jomo Kenyatta.",
 fact="In 2013 Britain paid about £20 million to 5,228 Kenyans tortured in detention camps."),
"af195741": dict(
 bluf="Ghanaian Prime Minister Kwame Nkrumah's Convention People's Party set out to win self-government from Britain through strikes, boycotts and elections. Britain conceded, and the Gold Coast became Ghana on 6 March 1957, the first sub-Saharan colony to win independence from European rule. The example set off a wave of independence across the continent, and Nkrumah became the leading voice of pan-Africanism before his rule turned authoritarian.",
 now="Ghana's independence opened the floodgates of African decolonisation, and its return to stable democracy since 1992 has made it a regional model.",
 story="Ghanaian nationalist Kwame Nkrumah founded the Convention People's Party in 1949 and called a campaign of strikes and boycotts. Britain jailed him in 1950; his party won the 1951 election while he was in prison, and Britain released him to lead the government. On 6 March 1957 the Gold Coast became independent Ghana, named after a medieval West African empire, with Nkrumah as prime minister.",
 why="Nkrumah's mass movement won elections Britain could ignore only by force, and a weakened Britain saw a gradual handover as the best way to keep influence.",
 then="Nkrumah championed pan-Africanism and backed independence movements across the continent. He made Ghana a one-party state in 1964 and was overthrown by the army in February 1966.",
 fact="Nkrumah named the country after the medieval Ghana Empire, which lay hundreds of miles away in today's Mali and Mauritania."),
"af196042": dict(
 bluf="France, Britain and Belgium each decided in 1960 that handing over power was cheaper than holding on, and seventeen African colonies became states in a single year. The new states inherited colonial borders, export economies built around raw materials and few trained administrators, and within months Congo was in crisis. Africa's map was fixed, and Cold War rivalry, coups and conflict followed alongside the building of new national identities and institutions.",
 now="Most of Africa's map, and many of its political challenges, date from this moment; the recent expulsion of French troops from the Sahel shows those post-independence ties are still contested.",
 story="In 1960 seventeen African countries won independence, among them Nigeria, Congo, Senegal, Madagascar, Somalia, Cameroon, Côte d'Ivoire and most of French West and Central Africa. In February British Prime Minister Harold Macmillan told South Africa's parliament that a 'wind of change' was blowing through the continent. Congo's crisis began within days of its independence on 30 June.",
 why="World War II had weakened the European powers and the United States and the UN pressed them to leave. Nationalist movements were strong. France chose rapid independence to keep influence through cooperation agreements.",
 then="Military coups swept the continent from the mid-1960s. France kept political, military and monetary ties with its former colonies, including the CFA franc.",
 fact="The UN admitted sixteen new African members in a single session in 1960, the largest intake in its history."),
"d-sharpeville": dict(
 bluf="The Pan Africanist Congress called Black South Africans to leave their passes at home and present themselves for arrest to break the pass laws. On 21 March 1960 police at Sharpeville fired on a crowd of thousands and killed 69, many shot in the back. The government banned the ANC and PAC, both turned to armed struggle in 1961, and apartheid became a target of worldwide condemnation.",
 now="Sharpeville ended the hope of peaceful change inside South Africa: the ANC and PAC were banned, turned to armed struggle in 1961 and stayed banned for thirty years, and the UN began its campaign against apartheid.",
 story="On 21 March 1960 thousands gathered at Sharpeville police station south of Johannesburg to protest against the laws forcing Black South Africans to carry passes. Police opened fire and killed 69 people, many of them shot in the back; about 180 were wounded.",
 why="PAC leader Robert Sobukwe had launched a campaign of mass arrest against the pass laws, and the police at Sharpeville faced a crowd many times their number.",
 trig="The PAC's anti-pass campaign of 21 March 1960.",
 then="The government declared a state of emergency and banned the ANC and PAC in April 1960. Both formed armed wings in 1961, and the UN Security Council condemned South Africa for the first time.",
 fact="21 March is Human Rights Day in South Africa and the UN's International Day for the Elimination of Racial Discrimination."),
"af196143": dict(
 bluf="Belgium, the United States and Congolese rivals wanted Congolese Prime Minister Patrice Lumumba gone, fearing he would bring the Soviet Union into Congo. Katangan secessionists shot him on 17 January 1961 with Belgian officers present, after Colonel Joseph Mobutu's soldiers had delivered him. Congo's elected government lost its leader seven months after independence, Mobutu ruled from 1965 to 1997 with Western backing, and Lumumba became the symbol of betrayed African independence.",
 now="The killing cleared the way for Congolese President Mobutu Sese Seko's 32-year rule and fixed the pattern of outside powers choosing Congo's rulers. Belgium apologised in 2022 and returned his only remains, a tooth, to his family.",
 story="Congolese Prime Minister Patrice Lumumba had denounced Belgian colonialism at the independence ceremony of 30 June 1960. Within weeks the army mutinied and Katanga seceded with Belgian support. Congolese President Joseph Kasa-Vubu dismissed Lumumba in September, Colonel Joseph Mobutu's soldiers arrested him, and he was flown to Katanga and shot on 17 January 1961 with Belgian officers present. The CIA had earlier plotted to poison him.",
 why="Belgium and the United States feared Lumumba would turn to the Soviet Union. Congolese rivals in Léopoldville and Katanga wanted him gone.",
 then="Congo slid into civil war. Mobutu seized power in 1965 and ruled for 32 years with Western support while looting the state.",
 fact="One of Lumumba's teeth, kept by a Belgian policeman who helped dissolve the body in acid, was returned to his family in 2022."),
"af196143b": dict(
 bluf="Tanzanian nationalist Julius Nyerere's Tanganyika African National Union set out to win independence from Britain by mass organising rather than arms. Britain agreed to a timetable, and the UN trust territory became independent on 9 December 1961 with Nyerere as prime minister. Nyerere built one of Africa's most unified states around Swahili and a single party, with economic results that stayed poor under his socialism.",
 now="Nyerere's choice of Swahili and a single national party made Tanzania one of the few African states without a major ethnic conflict since independence, a cohesion that still holds.",
 story="Tanzanian nationalist Julius Nyerere founded the Tanganyika African National Union in 1954 and built a mass party across the trust territory's 120 ethnic groups. TANU won the elections of 1958-59 and 1960. Tanganyika became independent on 9 December 1961 with Nyerere as prime minister; he became president of a republic in 1962.",
 why="TANU's mass support left Britain no alternative partner, and Britain accepted that the UN trust territory would become independent.",
 then="Tanganyika united with Zanzibar in April 1964 to form Tanzania. Nyerere's 1967 Arusha Declaration launched African socialism and, from 1973, forced villagisation.",
 fact="Nyerere is known in Tanzania as 'Mwalimu', Swahili for teacher, his profession before politics."),
"af196344": dict(
 bluf="Ethiopian Emperor Haile Selassie, Ghanaian President Kwame Nkrumah and thirty other heads of state wanted a body to defend their sovereignty, end colonial rule in the south and keep the peace among themselves. They founded the Organisation of African Unity in Addis Ababa in May 1963. It backed the liberation movements, froze the colonial borders and made non-interference a rule, which held the map together and shielded dictators from their neighbours.",
 now="The OAU became the African Union in 2002, and its 1964 rule that colonial borders stand still underpins Africa's map.",
 story="Thirty-two states founded the Organisation of African Unity in Addis Ababa on 25 May 1963, with Ethiopian Emperor Haile Selassie as host. Ghanaian President Kwame Nkrumah had argued for a union government; the majority chose a looser body of sovereign states. The charter committed members to non-interference and the end of colonial rule.",
 why="New states wanted their sovereignty defended, colonial rule ended in the rest of Africa and border disputes contained.",
 then="The OAU resolved in 1964 to respect the borders inherited at independence. It backed liberation movements in Angola, Mozambique, Rhodesia and South Africa.",
 fact="The OAU, and later the African Union, chose Addis Ababa because Ethiopia had kept its independence through the colonial era."),
"af196344b": dict(
 bluf="Kenyan nationalist Jomo Kenyatta's Kenya African National Union set out to end settler rule and take power at independence. Britain, convinced by the Mau Mau war that it could hold Kenya only by force, released Kenyatta in 1961 and handed over power on 12 December 1963. Kenyatta kept the colonial economy and the land distribution largely intact, and the land grievance still shapes Kenyan politics.",
 now="Kenyatta's settlement, which bought settler farms for a Kikuyu-led elite rather than redistributing them, fixed the land and ethnic grievances that have shaped every Kenyan election since 1992.",
 story="Britain had imprisoned Kenyan nationalist Jomo Kenyatta from 1953 to 1961 on disputed charges of leading Mau Mau. Released in August 1961, he led KANU to victory in the May 1963 election and became prime minister at independence on 12 December 1963. Kenya became a republic a year later with Kenyatta as president.",
 why="The Mau Mau war and the wider wave of decolonisation convinced Britain that settler rule was over.",
 then="Kenya became a one-party state under Kenyatta and then Daniel arap Moi until multiparty politics returned in December 1991.",
 fact="12 December, Jamhuri Day, is Kenya's national day; Kenyatta's son Uhuru was president from 2013 to 2022."),
"af196445": dict(
 bluf="Tanzanian President Julius Nyerere and Zanzibari leader Abeid Karume wanted to stabilise Zanzibar after its January 1964 revolution and keep Cold War rivalry off the islands. They merged the two states on 26 April 1964 as Tanzania. Nyerere built a cohesive nation on Swahili and a single party, and Zanzibar kept its own government in a union still argued over.",
 now="The union gave Tanzania a stable state with Swahili as a national language, and Zanzibar's semi-autonomous government remains a standing dispute in Tanzanian politics.",
 story="A revolution in Zanzibar overthrew the Arab-led sultanate on 12 January 1964, with thousands killed. Zanzibari leader Abeid Karume took power with support from leftists whom Western governments feared. Three months later Tanzanian President Julius Nyerere and Karume merged Tanganyika and Zanzibar as the United Republic of Tanzania.",
 why="Nyerere feared a Cuban-style state on his coast, and Karume needed a protector. Britain and the United States encouraged the union.",
 then="Tanzania became one of Africa's most stable states. Zanzibar kept its own president and assembly.",
 fact="The name combines TANganyika and ZANzibar."),
"d-rivonia": dict(
 bluf="The apartheid state put ANC leader Nelson Mandela and the ANC's underground command on trial for sabotage to destroy the resistance. In June 1964 eight defendants were sentenced to life imprisonment, most on Robben Island. The ANC's leadership was in prison for a generation, and Mandela's speech from the dock and his imprisonment made him the symbol of the anti-apartheid struggle worldwide.",
 now="The trial removed the ANC's leadership for 26 years and turned Mandela into the world's most famous political prisoner, which made his release in 1990 the condition for any settlement.",
 story="Police raided the ANC's underground headquarters at Liliesleaf Farm in Rivonia in July 1963 and captured its leaders. ANC leader Nelson Mandela, already serving a five-year sentence, was tried with them for sabotage. On 20 April 1964 he spoke for three hours from the dock, ending 'it is an ideal for which I am prepared to die'. On 12 June eight defendants, among them Walter Sisulu, were sentenced to life imprisonment.",
 why="The ANC had turned to sabotage after its banning in 1960, and the police found its command and its plans at the farm.",
 then="Mandela spent 27 years in prison. The ANC's leadership abroad rebuilt the movement in exile.",
 fact="The prosecution sought the death penalty; the judge gave life, and Mandela had prepared to be hanged."),
"af196746": dict(
 bluf="Nigeria's federal government under General Yakubu Gowon fought to prevent the Igbo southeast seceding as Biafra, and Biafran leader Odumegwu Ojukwu fought to keep it independent after massacres of Igbo in the north. The federal blockade starved Biafra into surrender in January 1970; estimates of the dead run from about 500,000 to more than two million, most from famine. Nigeria stayed whole, and Africa's colonial borders had been defended by force.",
 now="Biafra fixed the rule that Africa's colonial borders would be defended by force, and Igbo complaints of marginalisation and a revived separatist movement still shape Nigerian politics.",
 story="Two coups in 1966 and massacres of Igbo in northern Nigeria drove Igbo back to the southeast. On 30 May 1967 Biafran leader Odumegwu Ojukwu declared the region independent as Biafra. Nigerian federal forces under General Yakubu Gowon blockaded it, and images of starving children went round the world. Biafra surrendered on 15 January 1970.",
 why="Ethnic and regional rivalry in a federation built by British rule came first. The oil of the southeast made secession intolerable to Lagos.",
 trig="The declaration of Biafran independence on 30 May 1967.",
 then="Estimates of the dead range from about 500,000 to over 2 million, mostly from famine. Gowon declared 'no victor, no vanquished', and oil revenue centralised power in the federal government.",
 fact="Médecins Sans Frontières was founded in 1971 by French doctors who had worked in Biafra."),
"af196746b": dict(
 bluf="Tanzanian President Julius Nyerere wanted a self-reliant socialist nation built on village life rather than foreign capital or a private elite. The Arusha Declaration of February 1967 nationalised banks and industries, bound party leaders to a code against private wealth and, from 1973, moved millions into collective villages by force. The programme built national unity and a literate population and failed as an economy; Tanzania turned to market reforms in the 1980s.",
 now="Ujamaa's emphasis on Swahili and national unity helped make Tanzania one of Africa's most cohesive nations.",
 story="On 5 February 1967 Tanzanian President Julius Nyerere issued the Arusha Declaration committing Tanzania to Ujamaa, Swahili for 'familyhood'. The state nationalised banks, insurance and major industries and barred party leaders from holding shares or renting property. From 1973 the government moved millions of rural people into collective villages, often by force.",
 why="Nyerere believed an African socialism rooted in village life would build a united, self-reliant nation free of the dependence he saw in Kenya's model.",
 then="Ujamaa villages produced less than the farms they replaced, and food imports rose. Tanzania accepted IMF terms and market reforms in 1986, the year after Nyerere stepped down.",
 fact="Nyerere translated Shakespeare's 'Julius Caesar' and 'The Merchant of Venice' into Swahili."),
"af197447": dict(
 bluf="Ethiopian army officers formed the Derg in 1974 to force reform on a court that had hidden a famine and let prices rise. They deposed Emperor Haile Selassie on 12 September 1974, ended a monarchy that claimed descent from Solomon and turned to Marxism under Mengistu Haile Mariam. The Derg's Red Terror, its wars in Eritrea and Tigray and the famine of 1984 devastated Ethiopia until its fall in 1991.",
 now="The Derg's fall in 1991 brought a system of ethnic federalism that still shapes Ethiopia's politics and conflicts.",
 story="Ethiopian Emperor Haile Selassie had ruled since 1930. A famine in Wollo in 1972-74 killed tens of thousands while the court concealed it, and rising fuel and food prices set off strikes and army mutinies in early 1974. A committee of junior officers, the Derg, took control of the army and deposed the emperor on 12 September 1974.",
 why="The hidden famine discredited the court. Price rises and unpaid soldiers gave the mutinies their numbers.",
 trig="A television film of the Wollo famine, shown in Addis Ababa in September 1974 beside footage of the imperial court.",
 then="The emperor died in custody in August 1975. Derg leader Mengistu Haile Mariam took sole power in 1977, and the Red Terror of 1977-78 killed tens of thousands.",
 fact="Haile Selassie was driven from his palace in the back seat of a Volkswagen Beetle."),
"af197548": dict(
 bluf="Three Angolan movements had fought Portugal since 1961 and each wanted to rule when Portugal left. The MPLA under Agostinho Neto held Luanda on independence day, 11 November 1975, with Soviet arms and tens of thousands of Cuban troops, while UNITA and the FNLA fought it with American, South African and Zairean backing. Angola became one of the Cold War's longest proxy wars, with fighting until 2002 and perhaps half a million dead.",
 now="The MPLA has ruled Angola since 1975 on oil revenue. Angolan President José Eduardo dos Santos governed for 38 years, and his family's wealth became the symbol of oil-fuelled corruption.",
 story="Three movements, the MPLA, the FNLA and UNITA, had fought Portuguese rule since 1961. After Portugal's revolution of April 1974 they turned on each other. MPLA leader Agostinho Neto held Luanda with Soviet weapons and Cuban troops; UNITA leader Jonas Savimbi and the FNLA were backed by the United States, South Africa and Zaire. Portugal handed over on 11 November 1975 without naming a successor.",
 why="Portugal's army, exhausted by thirteen years of colonial war, overthrew its dictatorship in 1974 and left without an agreed government. Each outside power backed the movement nearest its interests.",
 then="The civil war ran with pauses until Savimbi was killed in February 2002. Cuban troops stayed until 1991; perhaps half a million people died.",
 fact="Cuba sent some 300,000 soldiers and civilians through Angola between 1975 and 1991."),
"af197548b": dict(
 bluf="FRELIMO under Samora Machel had fought Portugal since 1964 to end colonial rule in Mozambique. Portugal's own revolution of April 1974 ended the war, and Mozambique became independent on 25 June 1975 under Machel. The new state became a target of Rhodesia and South Africa, which armed RENAMO rebels in a civil war that killed about a million people before peace in 1992.",
 now="FRELIMO has governed Mozambique since independence, and its disputed 2024 election win set off deadly protests.",
 story="FRELIMO under Mozambican leader Samora Machel had fought Portuguese rule since 1964. After Portugal's Carnation Revolution of April 1974 the new government in Lisbon agreed to withdraw. Mozambique became independent on 25 June 1975 with Machel as president.",
 why="Portugal's colonial wars had exhausted its army, which overthrew the dictatorship in 1974.",
 then="Rhodesia and then South Africa armed RENAMO against FRELIMO's Marxist state. The civil war killed about a million people before the Rome peace accord of October 1992.",
 fact="Machel died in a 1986 plane crash on South African territory that many Mozambicans still blame on Pretoria."),
"d-soweto": dict(
 bluf="Black school students in Soweto marched on 16 June 1976 to end a decree forcing them to learn half their subjects in Afrikaans. Police fired on them; a photograph of the dying schoolboy Hector Pieterson went round the world, and hundreds died in the unrest that followed over months. A new generation turned against apartheid, thousands went abroad to join the ANC's armed wing, and world opinion hardened against South Africa.",
 now="The uprising turned a new generation against apartheid and drew world attention to it; June 16 is now Youth Day in South Africa.",
 story="On 16 June 1976 thousands of students marched through Soweto against a rule forcing schools to teach half their subjects in Afrikaans. Police opened fire. Photographer Sam Nzima's picture of the dying 12-year-old Hector Pieterson went round the world. Unrest spread across the country and hundreds were killed in the following months.",
 why="Apartheid's inferior 'Bantu education' came first. The Black Consciousness movement had given students a politics, and the Afrikaans decree made the rulers' language the classroom's.",
 trig="The Afrikaans decree of 1974 enforced in Soweto schools from the start of 1976.",
 then="Thousands of young people left to join the ANC in exile, which gave its armed wing a new generation. The government withdrew the Afrikaans rule.",
 fact="Hector Pieterson's sister Antoinette, running beside him in the photograph, later guided visitors at the museum named after him."),
"af197949": dict(
 bluf="Tanzanian President Julius Nyerere decided to remove Ugandan dictator Idi Amin after Amin's army invaded and annexed Tanzania's Kagera region in October 1978. Tanzanian forces and Ugandan exiles drove Amin's army back and took Kampala in April 1979. An African state had overthrown a neighbour's ruler with the OAU looking away, a breach of the non-interference rule that the African Union later turned into a right to intervene.",
 now="It is one of the few times an African country invaded a neighbour to remove a dictator, and the African Union later adopted a right to intervene against mass atrocities.",
 story="Ugandan dictator Idi Amin's army invaded Tanzania's Kagera region in October 1978. Tanzanian President Julius Nyerere's army, with Ugandan exiles including Yoweri Museveni's forces, counterattacked and captured Kampala on 11 April 1979. Amin fled to Libya and then Saudi Arabia.",
 why="Amin's invasion came first. Nyerere judged that expelling the troops would leave the threat in place and chose to remove Amin.",
 trig="The Ugandan invasion of Kagera in October 1978.",
 then="Uganda went through more coups and a civil war until Ugandan rebel leader Yoweri Museveni took power in January 1986.",
 fact="Amin's rule had killed an estimated 100,000 to 300,000 Ugandans; the OAU had elected him its chairman in 1975."),
"af198050": dict(
 bluf="Zimbabwean guerrilla leader Robert Mugabe's ZANU and its rival ZAPU fought to end white-minority rule in Rhodesia, which had declared independence from Britain in 1965 to avoid majority rule. The war forced the Lancaster House settlement of 1979, Mugabe won the 1980 election and Zimbabwe became independent in April 1980. Mugabe held power for 37 years; his land seizures from 2000 and hyperinflation wrecked the economy.",
 now="Zimbabwe's land seizures after 2000 and the hyperinflation that followed are the case every argument over land reform in southern Africa, including South Africa's, is conducted around. Mugabe was removed by his own army in November 2017.",
 story="Rhodesia's white minority under Prime Minister Ian Smith declared independence from Britain in November 1965 to avoid majority rule. A guerrilla war from 1972 forced negotiations at Lancaster House in London in 1979. Zimbabwean guerrilla leader Robert Mugabe's ZANU won the February 1980 election, and Zimbabwe became independent on 18 April 1980.",
 why="The guerrilla war and the loss of South African and Portuguese support left the white government without the means to continue.",
 then="Mugabe ruled for 37 years. His army killed thousands in Matabeleland in the 1980s, land seizures from 2000 cut farm output, and inflation reached billions of per cent in 2008.",
 fact="The country is named after Great Zimbabwe, a medieval stone city whose African origin colonial authorities denied."),
"af198451": dict(
 bluf="Drought struck northern Ethiopia in 1983-85 while the Derg government fought rebels in Tigray and Eritrea and moved peasants by force. Famine killed between 400,000 and a million people by the range of published estimates. BBC footage set off Band Aid and Live Aid, which transformed charity fundraising, and the government's use of aid and resettlement showed how a state at war can turn famine into a weapon.",
 now="Debates over aid in war zones, from Sudan to Gaza, still turn on the lesson that famine is usually political.",
 story="Drought struck Tigray and Wollo from 1983 while the Derg government under Mengistu Haile Mariam fought rebels there and restricted relief to rebel areas. BBC reporter Michael Buerk's film from Korem in October 1984 set off Band Aid that December and the Live Aid concerts of July 1985. Estimates of the dead run from 400,000 to one million.",
 why="Drought came first. War, the diversion of grain to the army and forced resettlement of some 600,000 people turned it into mass death.",
 then="Western charities adopted televised mass appeals as their model. Aid agencies later asked how much of their relief had fed the resettlement programme.",
 fact="Live Aid was watched by an estimated 1.5 billion people in 150 countries."),
"af199052": dict(
 bluf="South African President F. W. de Klerk decided that sanctions, internal resistance and a failing economy left white minority rule no future, and chose to negotiate while his government still held power. He legalised the ANC on 2 February 1990 and released ANC leader Nelson Mandela nine days later after 27 years in prison. Negotiations to end apartheid followed and produced the democratic election of 1994.",
 now="Mandela's release opened four years of negotiations that ended apartheid by agreement rather than war, which made South Africa the model for negotiated transitions from Northern Ireland to Colombia.",
 story="On 2 February 1990 South African President F. W. de Klerk told parliament he was legalising the ANC, the PAC and the Communist Party. On 11 February ANC leader Nelson Mandela walked out of Victor Verster prison near Paarl after 27 years. He addressed a crowd in Cape Town that evening.",
 why="Sanctions, resistance in the townships and a shrinking economy convinced de Klerk that apartheid could be ended on terms or lost outright. The Soviet collapse removed the fear of a communist ANC.",
 then="Four years of negotiations, with political violence that killed thousands, led to the election of 27 April 1994.",
 fact="Mandela was prisoner 46664 on Robben Island, and he stayed on a US terrorism watch list until 2008."),
"af199453": dict(
 bluf="Hutu extremists in Rwanda's interim government set out to kill the Tutsi minority and Hutu opponents after President Juvénal Habyarimana's plane was shot down on 6 April 1994. In about 100 days militias and soldiers killed about 800,000 people by the UN's count, above a million by a Rwandan government census, while UN peacekeepers stood aside. The Tutsi-led Rwandan Patriotic Front took Kigali in July, perpetrators fled into Congo and set off the Congo wars, and the world's failure produced the 'Responsibility to Protect' doctrine.",
 now="The world's failure to act led to the 'Responsibility to Protect' principle adopted by the UN in 2005; the flight of perpetrators into Congo set off wars there that continue, and Rwanda rebuilt under RPF leader Paul Kagame with rapid development and tight control.",
 story="Rwanda had been at civil war since 1990 between the Hutu-led government and the Tutsi-led Rwandan Patriotic Front, and a 1993 peace deal provided for power sharing. After Rwandan President Juvénal Habyarimana's plane was shot down on 6 April 1994, soldiers, the Interahamwe militia and radio broadcasts directed the killing of Tutsi and moderate Hutu across the country. The UN Security Council cut its peacekeeping force. The RPF under Paul Kagame took Kigali on 4 July 1994.",
 why="Ethnic categories hardened under Belgian rule and the civil war since 1990 came first. Extremists feared losing power under the 1993 peace deal, and the state had the radio, the militias and the lists.",
 trig="The shooting down of President Habyarimana's plane on 6 April 1994; who fired the missile is still disputed, with one inquiry blaming Hutu extremists and another the RPF.",
 then="Perpetrators and some two million Hutu refugees fled into Zaire, and Rwanda's pursuit of them began the Congo wars in 1996. The UN tribunal convicted 61 people. RPF leader Paul Kagame has governed Rwanda since; his supporters cite growth and order, his critics cite suppressed opposition and reprisal killings in 1994 and in Congo that the RPF denies.",
 myth="The genocide was a spontaneous outburst of ancient tribal hatred. The killing was organised by the state, with militias trained, weapons distributed and lists prepared in advance.",
 fact="Radio Mille Collines read out the names and hiding places of people to be killed; its founders were convicted of genocide in 2003."),
"af199453b": dict(
 bluf="The ANC and South African President F. W. de Klerk's National Party negotiated for four years to replace apartheid with majority rule without civil war. On 27 April 1994 South Africans of every race voted in the first democratic election; the ANC won 62 per cent and ANC leader Nelson Mandela was inaugurated president in May. Apartheid ended by agreement, and the economic inequality it built remained.",
 now="The negotiated transition became the model for ending conflict by settlement, and the inequality and corruption that followed remain South Africa's standing political questions into the 2020s.",
 story="Millions queued for hours on 27 April 1994, many voting for the first time. The ANC won 62 per cent, the National Party 20 per cent and the Inkatha Freedom Party 10 per cent. ANC leader Nelson Mandela, imprisoned for 27 years, was inaugurated president in Pretoria on 10 May 1994.",
 why="Four years of talks between the ANC and the National Party came first, pushed by sanctions, resistance and township violence that threatened civil war.",
 then="A Truth and Reconciliation Commission heard testimony on apartheid-era crimes from 1996, and the 1996 constitution became one of the most liberal in the world.",
 fact="27 April is Freedom Day; the ballot listed 19 parties with photographs of their leaders for voters who could not read."),
"af199654": dict(
 bluf="Rwanda and Uganda invaded Zaire in 1996 to destroy the Hutu forces that had fled there after the genocide and to replace Zairean President Mobutu Sese Seko with Laurent Kabila. They succeeded in 1997, then turned on Kabila in 1998 and drew nine countries into a war over territory and minerals. Estimates of the dead run into the millions, most from disease and hunger, and fighting over eastern Congo's minerals continues.",
 now="Fighting continues in eastern Congo, tied to minerals used in phones and electric cars.",
 story="Rwanda and Uganda invaded Zaire in October 1996 to break the Hutu militias in its refugee camps, and their ally Laurent Kabila took Kinshasa in May 1997 as Zairean President Mobutu Sese Seko fled. In August 1998 Rwanda and Uganda turned against Congolese President Laurent Kabila, and Angola, Zimbabwe and Namibia came to his defence. Armies and militias mined coltan, gold and diamonds to pay for the war.",
 why="The genocide's perpetrators and refugees in Zaire came first. Mobutu's state had collapsed, and eastern Congo's minerals paid every army.",
 then="One estimate, by the International Rescue Committee in 2008, put excess deaths at 5.4 million from 1998; other counts put the figure far lower, and all agree most died of disease and hunger. Eastern Congo is still fought over by dozens of armed groups, among them the M23 rebels, who seized Goma in January 2025; UN experts' reports of 2024 say Rwanda directs M23, which Rwanda denies.",
 fact="The second war drew in nine African armies, more than any conflict on the continent before or since."),
"d-trc": dict(
 bluf="South Africa's new government wanted the truth about apartheid-era crimes without trials that could break the negotiated transition. The Truth and Reconciliation Commission under Archbishop Desmond Tutu held public hearings from April 1996 and offered amnesty for full disclosure of political crimes; fewer than a thousand of about 7,000 applicants received it. Truth commissions from Latin America to Africa copied the model, and the trade of justice for peace is still argued over.",
 now="It became a model for truth commissions from Latin America to Africa, and debate over whether it traded justice for peace continues.",
 story="Chaired by Archbishop Desmond Tutu, the commission held public hearings from April 1996 at which thousands of victims told their stories. Perpetrators could apply for amnesty if they fully disclosed politically motivated crimes; fewer than a thousand of about 7,000 applicants received it. The final report of October 1998 blamed the apartheid state most and also criticised the ANC.",
 why="The negotiated transition left the old security forces intact, and the new government needed the record without prosecutions that could destabilise the country.",
 then="Few of those refused amnesty were ever prosecuted. Reparations paid to victims fell short of what the commission recommended.",
 fact="Tutu wept openly at the first hearing and was criticised for it; he said the stories left him no choice."),
"af200255": dict(
 bluf="African leaders wanted a body that could act where the OAU had stood aside, after Rwanda in 1994 had shown the cost of strict non-interference. The African Union launched in Durban in July 2002 with a charter right to intervene in member states against genocide, war crimes and crimes against humanity. It has mandated peacekeeping in Somalia and suspended members after coups, while lacking the money to enforce its decisions.",
 now="The Union replaced an organisation that could not intervene with one that can in principle, and it has since mandated peacekeeping in Somalia and suspended members after coups, while lacking the money to enforce its decisions.",
 story="The African Union launched in Durban on 9 July 2002 to replace the Organisation of African Unity. Its Constitutive Act gave it the right to intervene in a member state in cases of genocide, war crimes and crimes against humanity, a break with the OAU's non-interference rule.",
 why="The OAU had failed to prevent atrocities such as Rwanda's, and leaders wanted stronger cooperation and peacekeeping. Libyan leader Muammar Gaddafi pushed for a union and paid much of the bill.",
 then="It has sent peacekeepers to Somalia, Sudan and elsewhere and suspended members after coups. The Sahel juntas of 2020-23 defied its suspensions.",
 fact="The AU became a permanent member of the G20 in September 2023."),
"af200356": dict(
 bluf="Rebels from Darfur's non-Arab groups attacked government posts in 2003 to end their region's marginalisation, and Sudanese President Omar al-Bashir's government chose to crush them with Arab militias, the Janjaweed. The militias and the army burned villages, killed and raped; about 300,000 died by a UN estimate of 2008 and more than 2 million were displaced. The International Criminal Court charged a sitting head of state for the first time, and the Janjaweed became the Rapid Support Forces now fighting Sudan's army.",
 now="Janjaweed leaders formed the RSF, now fighting Sudan's army in a war marked by fresh mass killings in Darfur, including after the fall of El Fasher in 2025.",
 story="In 2003 rebels from Darfur's Fur, Zaghawa and Masalit communities attacked government posts. Sudanese President Omar al-Bashir's government armed Arab militias, the Janjaweed, which with air and army support burned villages, killed and raped across Darfur. A UN estimate of 2008 put the dead at around 300,000 and the displaced at more than 2 million; the Sudanese government put deaths at about 10,000.",
 why="Darfur's non-Arab groups rebelled against marginalisation by Khartoum. The government, with its army tied up in the south, chose militias to crush them.",
 then="The ICC issued a warrant for al-Bashir in 2009 and added genocide in 2010, the first charges against a sitting head of state. Janjaweed units became the Rapid Support Forces in 2013 under Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo.",
 fact="Al-Bashir travelled to several ICC member states after the warrant, and none arrested him."),
"af201157": dict(
 bluf="Southern Sudanese leaders wanted a state of their own after two civil wars with Khartoum, and the 2005 peace deal gave them a referendum. Nearly 99 per cent voted to secede, and South Sudan became independent on 9 July 2011 under President Salva Kiir. Within two years a power struggle between Kiir and Vice President Riek Machar became a civil war, and the state built by armed struggle had few institutions to hold it.",
 now="South Sudan remains one of the world's most fragile states, and renewed fighting and the arrest of Vice President Machar in 2025 threatened its peace deal.",
 story="Two civil wars between Sudan's government and southern rebels, from 1955 to 1972 and 1983 to 2005, ended in a peace deal that promised a referendum. In January 2011 nearly 99 per cent of southern voters chose secession. South Sudan became independent on 9 July 2011 with former rebel commander Salva Kiir as president.",
 why="Decades of war and discrimination by Khartoum against the mostly non-Arab, non-Muslim south came first. Oil in the south gave both sides something to fight for.",
 then="A power struggle between President Salva Kiir and Vice President Riek Machar became civil war in December 2013, killing an estimated 400,000 people before a 2018 peace deal.",
 fact="South Sudan is the world's newest widely recognised state and the UN's 193rd member."),
"af201458": dict(
 bluf="The Ebola virus spread from a Guinean village in December 2013 into Liberia and Sierra Leone, where health systems weakened by years of war could neither trace nor isolate it. By 2016 there had been about 28,600 cases and over 11,300 deaths, with the World Health Organization slow to declare an emergency. The epidemic produced faster international outbreak responses, a WHO emergency fund and the first licensed Ebola vaccine.",
 now="An Ebola vaccine was approved in 2019, and the epidemic offered lessons, often unheeded, for COVID-19.",
 story="The outbreak began with a child in Meliandou in Guinea in December 2013 and spread to Liberia and Sierra Leone in 2014, the first Ebola epidemic in crowded cities. The World Health Organization declared an international emergency in August 2014. By 2016 there had been about 28,600 cases and over 11,300 deaths.",
 why="Health systems broken by civil war in Liberia and Sierra Leone came first. The virus reached cities for the first time, and burial customs spread it.",
 then="A vaccine trial in Guinea in 2015 proved effective, and the rVSV vaccine was licensed in 2019. WHO created an emergency programme and a contingency fund.",
 fact="Health workers made up about 10 per cent of the dead in Liberia and Sierra Leone, countries that had fewer than 300 doctors between them."),
"af202059": dict(
 bluf="Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed sent the army into Tigray in November 2020 to remove the Tigray People's Liberation Front, which had run Ethiopia until 2018; the TPLF fought to keep its region. Two years of war with Eritrean troops on the government side killed hundreds of thousands by estimates published since 2022, many from famine, and ended in a peace deal in November 2022. Ethiopia's standing as a rising power fell, and the strain on its ethnic federal system spread to Amhara and Oromia.",
 now="It stalled Ethiopia's economic rise, and tensions between Ethiopia and Eritrea, including over Ethiopia's demand for access to the sea, raise fears of a new war.",
 story="The Tigray People's Liberation Front had led Ethiopia's ruling coalition until Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed took office in 2018, and it held a regional election in 2020 that Addis Ababa called illegal. On 4 November 2020 Abiy, the 2019 Nobel Peace laureate, sent federal troops into Tigray; the government said the TPLF had attacked a federal army base, and the TPLF said the attack pre-empted a planned federal assault. Eritrean forces joined the government side, and aid access to Tigray was cut for most of 2021-22.",
 why="A struggle over power between Abiy's government and the TPLF, which had dominated the state for 27 years, came first. Eritrean President Isaias Afwerki had fought the TPLF since 1998 and wanted it broken.",
 trig="The TPLF's regional election of September 2020 and the fighting at the army's Northern Command bases on 3-4 November 2020.",
 then="The Pretoria peace agreement of 2 November 2022 ended the fighting. Estimates of the dead published since 2022 run to hundreds of thousands, most from hunger and lack of medical care; the government disputes the higher figures. Tensions with Eritrea and fighting in Amhara and Oromia continued.",
 fact="Abiy received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2019 for making peace with Eritrea, whose army then fought beside his in Tigray."),
"af202160": dict(
 bluf="Soldiers in Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger seized power between 2020 and 2023 to end civilian governments they blamed, with French troops, for failing to stop jihadist insurgencies. The juntas expelled French forces, brought in Russian military contractors and left the regional bloc ECOWAS in 2025. The Sahel's alliances were reshaped, and jihadist violence kept rising, with the region recording the most terrorism deaths in the world by 2024.",
 now="Jihadist violence in the Sahel has kept growing, making it the region with the most terrorism deaths in the world, and in 2025 militants blockaded fuel supplies to Mali's capital.",
 story="Soldiers took power in Mali in August 2020 and again in May 2021, in Burkina Faso in January and September 2022 and in Niger in July 2023. Each junta blamed the civilian government and French troops for failing to stop jihadist insurgents who had killed thousands and displaced millions. France withdrew its forces from Mali in 2022, Burkina Faso in 2023 and Niger by December 2023.",
 why="The insurgencies came first; by 2020 jihadist groups held much of rural Mali and Burkina Faso. Anti-French anger gave the officers public support, and Russia offered contractors without conditions.",
 then="Russian contractors of the Wagner Group, later the Africa Corps, deployed in Mali from 2021. The three juntas formed the Alliance of Sahel States in 2023 and left ECOWAS in January 2025. The juntas say they have restored sovereignty; their critics cite rising deaths and postponed elections.",
 fact="The three states together cover an area larger than Western Europe and had a combined population of about 70 million at the time of the coups."),
"af202361": dict(
 bluf="Sudanese army chief General Abdel Fattah al-Burhan and Rapid Support Forces commander General Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo had seized power together in 2021 and fought from April 2023 over who would control the state as the RSF's integration into the army was negotiated. Neither has won; the war has displaced more than 10 million people by UN counts of 2024, brought confirmed famine to Darfur and drawn in outside backers for each side. Sudan's state has fractured, with the army in the east and the RSF holding Darfur.",
 now="It is one of the world's worst humanitarian crises and a regional proxy contest: the United Arab Emirates is accused of backing the RSF, which it denies, while Egypt and others back the army.",
 story="On 15 April 2023 fighting broke out in Khartoum between the army under General Abdel Fattah al-Burhan and the Rapid Support Forces of General Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo, known as Hemedti; the two had seized power together in a 2021 coup. Each side says the other fired first. The RSF took most of Khartoum and Darfur; the army retook the capital in 2025. In Darfur the RSF and allied militias carried out ethnic killings of Masalit and others, which the RSF denies ordering.",
 why="The two generals fought over control of the state and the RSF's integration into the army as a transition to civilian rule was being negotiated. Gold and outside backers gave each side the means to continue.",
 trig="Fighting at RSF and army bases in Khartoum on 15 April 2023.",
 then="More than 10 million people were displaced by UN counts of 2024, famine was confirmed in parts of Darfur in August 2024, and the RSF took El Fasher in October 2025 amid reports of mass killings that the RSF disputes. The army says it is defending the state against a militia; the RSF says it is fighting the old regime's officers.",
 fact="Khartoum's fighting began with the RSF's own bases inside the capital, which the 2021 coup partners had left unmerged."),
}
